course: myths
lesson: 6
title: Growing, skin, hair and food
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The party is over, there is cake on the ceiling, and Mum groans: "No more sweets, they're bouncing off the walls on all that sugar!" Every grown-up in the room nods.
explain: But scientists tested it: some children got sugar, others a sugar-free drink, and not even their parents knew who had which. Their behaviour came out the same, and it was the party, not the sugar, that made everyone wild.
explain: Body myths spread because something looks true: veins look blue, so "blood is blue"; stubble feels rough, so "shaving makes hair thicker"; skin shrinks after death, so "hair keeps growing".
explain: And sometimes the strange claim wins: eat mountains of carrots and your skin really can turn a bit orange, and you really are a little taller in the morning than at bedtime.
explain: So the trick is not to laugh at everything or believe everything, but to ask: what is really going on, and how could I check it?
fact: Careful studies, where neither children nor parents knew who had sugar, found sugar did not make children hyperactive (wildly overactive); excitement at parties is the more likely cause, and the secret mattered, because parents who think a child had sugar tend to notice wilder behaviour.
fact: Spots come mainly from hormones, skin and genes; one chocolate bar will not cause a breakout, though very sugary diets may matter for some people.
fact: Shaving does not change how thick, dark or fast hair grows; cut hair just has a blunt tip that feels coarser.
fact: Hair and nails do not grow after death; the skin dries and shrinks, so they only look longer.
fact: Blood is always red: bright red with oxygen and darker red without; veins look blue because of the way skin scatters light.
fact: Eating huge amounts of carrots really can turn the skin slightly orange; it is called carotenaemia, it is harmless and it fades.
fact: Each hair root grows one hair, so pulling out a grey hair does not make two grow back.
fact: The slogan that breakfast is "the most important meal" was spread partly by cereal advertising; breakfast helps many children concentrate, but scientists debate how special it is.
fact: Frozen vegetables are frozen soon after picking and keep their vitamins well; they can be as healthy as fresh ones that have travelled for days.
fact: Growth hormone is released during deep sleep, and you are slightly taller in the morning because your spine stretches out while you lie flat.
fact: When everyone blames one thing, like sugar, it is worth checking whether something else, like a party, happens at the same time.
exercise: Mum says the party sweets make children "bounce off the walls". What did careful studies find? | Sugar didn't change behaviour; the party excitement did | Sugar makes children twice as active for about an hour afterwards | Only sweets with colours cause it | Sugar makes children sleepy at once
exercise: In the sugar studies, why did it matter that parents didn't know who had sugar? | So their expectations couldn't change what they noticed | So the children would get a nice surprise at the party | So nobody could steal the sweets | So the drinks tasted the same to grown-ups
exercise: Big cousin blames a chocolate bar for her spots. What mainly causes spots? | Hormones, skin and genes | One bar of chocolate | Not washing for a single day | Touching chocolate wrappers
exercise: Dad warns that shaving makes hair grow back thicker and darker. What's true? | Shaving doesn't change hair; the blunt tip just feels rough | Each shave makes the hair twice as thick | Shaving turns light hairs dark forever | Shaved hair grows back twice as fast
exercise: The spooky story says hair and nails keep growing after death. Why do they look longer? | The skin dries and shrinks back around them | They grow faster once the heart has stopped beating | The air in the coffin makes them stretch over time | They really do keep growing for about a month
exercise: Leah says blood is blue inside you and turns red in air. What colour is it really? | Always red: bright with oxygen, darker without | Blue inside your body, and red only once it's outside | Purple everywhere in the body | Clear until it touches skin
exercise: Why do veins look blue through your skin? | Because of the way skin scatters light | Because the blood inside them is really blue | Because veins are coloured blue on the inside | Because they are full of air instead of blood
exercise: Arjun says eating loads of carrots can turn you orange. Is he right? | Yes, it can turn skin slightly orange, harmlessly | No, that's a total myth | Yes, and the orange colour stays on your skin forever | Only if you eat carrots with orange juice
exercise: Granny says pull out one grey hair and two grow back. What's true? | Each root grows one hair; you just go greyer with age | Two roots open for every hair pulled | Pulled hairs come back grey and doubled | Grey hairs grow back as white feathers
exercise: Who helped make "breakfast is the most important meal of the day" famous? | Cereal advertising | A law passed by the government | Every scientist in the world agreeing | A famous footballer's diary
exercise: What do scientists say about breakfast? | It helps many children concentrate, but how special it is is debated | It is proven to be the only meal that matters | Your brain switches off completely without it | It is bad for children's concentration
exercise: Grandpa says frozen vegetables are rubbish and fresh is always healthier. What's true? | Frozen veg is frozen soon after picking and keeps its vitamins well | Freezing destroys all the vitamins | Frozen veg is made from leftover plastic | Fresh veg always has double the vitamins of frozen veg, whatever happens
exercise: Ivy's mum says you grow taller in your sleep. Is there truth in it? | Yes, growth hormone is released in deep sleep | No, children only grow while they are running about | No, sleeping flat actually makes you a bit shorter | Yes, but only if you sleep standing up like a horse
exercise: Why are you slightly taller in the morning than in the evening? | Your spine stretches out while you lie flat all night | Your bones grow a whole centimetre every single night while you sleep | Your feet swell up in the night | Your hair stands up after sleeping
exercise: Everyone blames sugar for wild party kids. What's the lesson? | The thing everyone blames isn't always the cause | Whatever most people say about children is always true | Parents are always wrong about children | Parties are bad for children
test: Your sister says: "My veins are BLUE, look! Proves blood's blue!" How could you check? | Look at blood in a sealed tube at a blood test: it's dark red | Cut a vein to look, carefully | Hold your arm in the sun until it turns red | Ask her to drink blue juice
test: "Mate, ate a Mars bar yesterday and got 3 spots today. Chocolate did it, 100%." Best answer? | Spots are mostly hormones and skin, one bar won't do that | Yes, each bar of chocolate gives you exactly three new spots | Chocolate spots go away if you eat more | Only dark chocolate is safe for skin
test: What would happen if you measured your height first thing in the morning and again at bedtime? | You'd be a tiny bit taller in the morning | You'd be exactly the same height both times | You'd be a little bit taller at bedtime instead | You'd be shorter in the morning after lying down
test: Grandad keeps plucking grey hairs to stop them. Granny warns two will grow back. A kind thing to say? | "It's one hair per root, Grandad, you're just getting wiser!" | "Granny's right, stop or you'll be bald." | "Pluck them all, they won't come back." | "Grey hair is a disease, see a doctor."
test: In a horror film, a body in a coffin has long new fingernails after a year. What's really going on? | The skin shrank back, so the nails only look longer | Nails really do grow faster after death, for weeks and weeks | The coffin air made them grow | The film is right, nails grow for years
test: You laughed at Arjun's carrot claim, then found out it's real. What's the best thing to say? | "I thought it was a myth, but you're right, you win!" | "Still a myth, I don't care what the experiment says, I'm right." | "It only works in cartoons." | Say nothing and hope he forgets
test: The supermarket has bags of frozen peas and some "fresh" peas that came from far away days ago. Which might have more vitamins? | The frozen peas could easily have as many or more | The fresh ones always win, because frozen peas have no vitamins | Neither has any vitamins at all | Only the bag with the bigger price
test: A cereal box says "Breakfast is the most important meal!" What's a smart question to ask? | Who is saying this, and are they selling something? | How many colours are on the box? | Why don't they say that lunch is the most important meal instead? | Is the box made of recycled paper?
test: "Shaved my leg hair last week and it's all stubbly and thick now, told ya!" What's really happening? | The hairs have blunt cut ends that feel rough, not thicker | Shaving doubled the number of hairs | The hairs got darker and twice as thick because of the sharp razor | The leg grew new roots after shaving
test: Your little brother is wild at a birthday party, sugar-free drinks and all. What does that suggest? | The excitement of the party, not sugar, makes kids hyper | Sugar-free drinks are secretly full of sugar | He must have eaten sugar somewhere | Sugar-free drinks make kids even wilder
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colour of human blood within the body? | It is invariably red, of a darker shade when deficient in oxygen | It is blue within the veins and becomes red only upon exposure to air | It is colourless until it comes into contact with the skin | It is purple throughout the circulation
exam: It is given that children were administered either sugar or a sugar-free substitute, neither the children nor their parents being informed which. Select the response consistent with the findings. | No difference in behaviour attributable to sugar was observed | Sugar produced a marked increase in hyperactivity | The sugar-free substitute produced hyperactivity | The parents correctly identified every child given sugar
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a shaved hair is perceived to be coarser upon regrowth. | The severed hair possesses a blunt tip | Shaving increases the thickness of each hair | Shaving accelerates the rate of hair growth | Shaving doubles the number of follicles
exam: Ascertain which of the following claims concerning the human body is, notwithstanding its improbable appearance, correct. | Excessive consumption of carrots may tint the skin orange | Hair and nails continue to grow after death | Removal of one grey hair occasions the growth of two in its place | Blood is blue until exposed to air
